.TH CLIPMUNGE 5 "2026-09-09" "clipmunge" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
clipmunge \- Lua configuration for clipmunge(1)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/clipmunge/config.lua
.PP
Defaults to
.I ~/.config/clipmunge/config.lua
when XDG_CONFIG_HOME is unset, empty or relative.
.SH DESCRIPTION
The config calls
.B clipmunge.rule
once per rule. Rules run in declaration order against the incoming plain text.
The first valid replacement wins; rules do not chain.
A missing config file is an error.
.PP
Only install trusted configs: they can choose the program used for
notifications. See SECURITY in
.BR clipmunge (1).
.SH RULES
.B clipmunge.rule
takes a table:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
clipmunge.rule {
  name = "tickets",
  match = [[^([A-Z]+)-(\ed+)$]],
  when = "plain-only",
  handler = function(_, kind, number)
    local id = kind .. "-" .. number
    return clipmunge.link("https://tracker.example.com/" .. id, id)
  end,
}
.EE
.RE
.TP
.B name
Required string identifying the rule in diagnostics.
.TP
.B match
Required regular expression, applied to plain text with leading and trailing
whitespace trimmed. Empty or unreadable plain text is skipped.
.IP
Uses Rust regex syntax. Alternation and bounded repetition such as
.B \ed{6,9}
are supported; lookaround and backreferences are not.
Unicode support includes
.BR \ed ,
.BR \ew ,
.B \es
and case folding. Script classes such as
.B \ep{Greek}
are unavailable. Patterns are compiled when the config loads.
.IP
Lua's
.B string.match
uses Lua patterns, which have different syntax.
.TP
.B when
.B "always"
(default), or
.BR "plain-only" .
The latter skips selections with successfully read payloads of
.BR text/html ,
.BR chromium/x-source-url ,
.B text/uri-list
or
.BR text/rtf .
.TP
.B handler
Required function. Receives the incoming selection, then capture groups
starting at group 1. An unmatched optional group is
.BR nil .
Use
.B _
for the first argument if the handler only needs captures.
.SS Incoming selection
Read-only userdata, valid for the duration of the handler call.
Accessing a retained reference after the call fails.
.TP
.B incoming:text()
The original plain text as UTF-8, without the trimming used for matching.
The first present payload in the text/plain family is used; if it is invalid
UTF-8, the selection is not passed to rules.
.TP
.BI incoming:get( mime )
The payload as a Lua byte string, or
.B nil
if unavailable. Non-UTF-8 bytes are preserved. MIME names match exactly.
.TP
.BI incoming:has( mime )
Whether a payload is present, without copying it. MIME names match exactly.
.TP
.B incoming:mimes()
List of available MIME types.
.PP
The text/plain family, in preference order, is
.BR text/plain;charset=utf-8 ,
.BR text/plain ,
.BR UTF8_STRING ,
.B STRING
and
.BR TEXT .
The daemon also reads
.BR text/html ,
.BR chromium/x-source-url ,
.B text/uri-list
and
.BR text/rtf .
Other advertised types are skipped. Reads are limited to 256 KiB per flavour,
500 ms per flavour and one second per selection; failed or oversized reads
may leave a flavour unavailable. Such a flavour is also absent from
.BR incoming:has() ,
.B incoming:mimes()
and the
.B plain-only
check.
.SH HANDLER RESULTS
.TP
.B nil
Decline and try the next rule. If no rule produces a replacement, leave the
clipboard unchanged.
.TP
a string
Replace plain text and drop other flavours.
.TP
a table
Map MIME names to payload strings.
.B text
is shorthand for the plain-text family;
.B notify
contains an optional notification message.
An empty table or one containing only a notification is invalid.
Plain text and notifications must be valid UTF-8; other MIME payloads may
contain arbitrary bytes. Avoid combining
.B text
with explicit text/plain-family keys, since their overwrite order is unspecified.
.PP
Only the returned flavours are published; incoming flavours are not inherited.
Escape any HTML assembled by hand with
.BR clipmunge.html_escape .
.PP
Handler errors and invalid results are logged, then the next rule is tried.
A notification is sent only after the replacement reaches the clipboard.
.SH LIBRARY
.TP
.BI clipmunge.link( url "[, " text ])
Return a table containing plain text, an escaped HTML link and
.BR chromium/x-source-url .
\fItext\fR defaults to \fIurl\fR.
.TP
.BI clipmunge.html_escape( s )
Escape text for use in HTML text or quoted attribute values.
.TP
.BI clipmunge.url.strip_params( url "[, " patterns ])
Return a cleaned URL and the list of removed parameter names, or
.B nil
if nothing matched.
.IP
Parameter names match case-insensitively. A trailing
.B *
matches a prefix, so
.B utm_*
covers all names with that prefix. Names are not percent-decoded.
Retained parameters keep their bytes and order; fragments are preserved.
Empty query segments are discarded when a parameter is removed.
If all parameters are removed, the
.B ?
is removed too.
.IP
\fIpatterns\fR replaces the default list. To extend it, copy
.B clipmunge.url.default_junk
and append entries, then pass that table explicitly. Editing
.B clipmunge.url.default_junk
alone does not change the defaults used by calls without a second argument.
.TP
.B clipmunge.url.default_junk
The default parameter patterns as a Lua table, including
.BR utm_* ,
.BR fbclid ,
.BR gclid ,
.BR msclkid ,
.BR igshid ,
.BR si ,
.BR yclid
and
.BR spm .
Patterns apply to every domain and may remove legitimate parameters on some
sites. Use a custom list or restrict the rule's URL pattern when needed.
.SH SETTINGS
.B clipmunge.settings
takes a table. Calls during config execution merge settings by key; the final
values are read after the config finishes. Calls from handlers do not change
active settings. Unknown keys produce a warning at load time.
.TP
.B notify_command
Non-empty argument list for notifications. Default:
.PP
.RS
.EX
{ "notify-send", "-a", "clipmunge", "clipmunge", "{}" }
.EE
.RE
.IP
Each occurrence of
.B {}
in arguments after the executable name is replaced with the handler's
notification text. The executable name itself is not substituted.
Arguments are passed without a shell. No command runs unless a handler
returns a notification and notifications are enabled.
.TP
.B secret_mimes
MIME names that cause an offer to be skipped before any content is read.
Default:
.PP
.RS
.EX
{ "x-kde-passwordManagerHint" }
.EE
.RE
.IP
The list replaces the defaults. An empty table disables this check.
Names match case-insensitively; only their presence is checked, not their values.
This also prevents content diagnostics under
.BR \-\-debug .
Applications may omit the hint; it does not cover all passwords.
.SH EXAMPLE
.RS 4
.EX
clipmunge.rule {
  name = "detrack",
  match = [[^(https?://\eS+)$]],
  handler = function(_, url)
    local clean, dropped = clipmunge.url.strip_params(url)
    if not clean then return nil end
    local out = clipmunge.link(clean)
    out.notify = "Dropped " .. table.concat(dropped, ", ")
    return out
  end,
}
.EE
.RE
.SH MATCHING AND REPEATED COPIES
Use specific patterns. A catch-all such as
.B (?s)^(.+)$
can match any nonempty plain text, including secrets whose source omits the hint.
Passwords can also match more specific patterns.
.PP
Published selections carry
.B application/x-clipmunge
and are ignored by the daemon. Rules are not reapplied within a pass.
If another application copies the result without preserving that marker,
rules can see it again. Return
.B nil
when there is nothing to change to avoid unnecessary rewrites and notifications.
.SH SPLITTING THE CONFIG
.B require
initially searches beside the config. For a symlinked config, it searches
both the given and resolved directories, using
.B ?.lua
and
.BR ?/init.lua .
C modules are disabled.
.PP
Changes directly in the watched directories trigger reloads. Subdirectories
are not watched recursively, and the watched directories are fixed at startup.
See RELOADING in
.BR clipmunge (1).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR clipmunge (1)
